The '''history of Europe''' can be traced back to the appearance of the [[early European modern humans|first modern humans]] identified as ancestors of contemporary Europeans about 37,000 years ago during the [[Paleolithic Europe|Paleolithic]]. [[Early European Farmers]] migrating from [[Anatolia#Prehistoric Anatolia|Anatolia]] spread agriculture across the continent during the [[Neolithic Europe|Neolithic]] (7000–2000 BC), a period that also included [[Metallurgy during the Copper Age in Europe|early metallurgy]]. Starting around 4000 BC, westward migrations of [[Western Steppe Herders|steppe pastoralists]] spread [[Proto-Indo-Europeans|Indo-European]] languages. The rise of the [[Polis|city-states]] of [[ancient Greece]] defines the beginning of [[classical antiquity]] (c. 800 BC-AD 500). The [[Hellenistic period]] (323–30 BC) began with the death of [[Alexander the Great]] and ended with the [[Principate|Roman Empire]]'s control of the [[Mare Nostrum|entire Mediterranean basin]]. [[Early Christianity|Christianity emerged]] under Roman rule in the 1st century AD. The [[Migration Period]] of the [[Germanic peoples]] began in the late 4th century AD.

[[File:Tommaso da modena, ritratti di domenicani (Ugo di Provenza) 1352 150cm, treviso, ex convento di san niccolò, sala del capitolo (cropped).jpg|thumb|upright|Detail of a portrait of Cardinal [[Hugh of Saint-Cher]] wearing [[Glasses#Invention|spectacles]] (1352).{{efn|text=The portrait was painted by [[Tommaso da Modena]]. Tommaso trained in Venice and worked mostly in Northern Italy.}}]]
The [[fall of the Western Roman Empire|abdication of the last Western Roman Emperor]] in 476 traditionally marks the beginning of the [[Middle Ages]] (c. 500–1500). While the [[Eastern Roman Empire|Eastern Roman ("Byzantine") Empire]] with its capital in [[Constantinople]] continued for another 1000 years, the former lands of the [[Western Roman Empire]] fragmented into the [[barbarian kingdoms]]. Beginning in the 7th century the [[Early_Muslim_conquests#Arab–Byzantine_naval_warfare|Mediterranean became a battleground]] between the Byzantines and [[Rashidun Caliphate|Muslims]]. The 8th-century [[Umayyad conquest of Hispania]] established [[Al-Andalus]]. The Rome-based [[Latin Church#History|Latin Church]] proclaimed the King of the [[Franks#Carolingian kingdom (751–987)|Franks]], [[Charlemagne]], as [[Charlemagne#Coronation|"Emperor of the Romans"]] in the early 9th century and increasingly competitive [[Christianization of the Slavs|missionary efforts]] by the [[Latin West]] and Byzantines resulted in the division of the [[Slavic peoples]] between [[Medieval Latin|Latin]] and [[Cyrillic]] use. [[Norsemen]] [[Siege of Paris (885–886)|raided]], [[Cnut's invasion of England|invaded]], and [[Slavery in medieval Europe#Vikings|trafficked slaves]] across Europe during the [[Viking Age]]. During the [[High Middle Ages]] (c. 1000–1300), the Latin Church [[East–West Schism|split with the Eastern Orthodox Church]], was [[History of the papacy (1048–1257)#Electoral reform|convulsed by reform]] and [[Investiture Controversy|entangled in conflict with secular monarchs]], called for the [[Crusades]], and provided key support for [[Lombard League|northern Italian city-states]] achieving [[Peace of Constance#Terms|independence from the Holy Roman Empire]]. The [[History of European universities#The first European universities|oldest university]] currently in continuous operation in the world was established in the 12th century amid rapid population and urban growth that continued until the end of the 13th century. Despite a [[Medieval demography#Late Middle Ages|decline in population]] caused by the [[Great Famine of 1315–1317|Great Famine]] and the [[Black Death]] in the 14th century, Europe experienced a cultural [[Renaissance]] in the 15th century.

[[Early modern Europe]] began with 15th-century revolutions in both [[Printing press#Mass production and spread in Europe|printing]] and [[Age of Discovery#Technology: Ship design and the compass|seafaring]]. Printing changed how [[Printing press#Science|knowledge was created, preserved and disseminated]] while the [[Age of Discovery]] led to extensive [[Analysis of European colonialism and colonization|colonization]], the [[Atlantic slave trade]] and the [[Columbian exchange]]. The 16th-century [[Reformation]] challenged the hegemony of the [[Catholic Church#Age of Discovery and Counter-Reformation|Catholic Church]] and precipitated [[European wars of religion|over a century of religious wars]]. With leadership from Italian [[astronomer]] [[Galileo Galilei]] and English [[mathematical physicist]] [[Isaac Newton]], modern science emerged during the [[Scientific Revolution]] of the 17th century.

The [[Industrial Revolution]] and [[Age of Revolution|waves of liberal and nationalist upheaval]] shaped the [[long nineteenth century|long 19th century]] dominated by the five great powers of the [[Concert of Europe]]. [[Triple Alliance (1882)|Two rival]] [[Triple Entente|military alliances]] fought [[World War I]] (1914–1918), which ended with multi-ethnic empires fragmented into [[nation state]]s. [[Nazi Germany]]'s [[invasion of Poland]] launched [[World War II]] (1939–1945), and as its armies overran Europe the regime systematically perpetrated [[the Holocaust]]. During the [[Cold War]] (1947–1991), Europe was divided between a [[United States#Cold War and social revolution (1945–1991)|U.S.]]-led [[Western bloc]] of countries and the [[NATO]] military alliance and the [[Soviet Union|Soviet]]-led [[Eastern bloc]] and the [[Warsaw Pact]]. The Western bloc's [[decolonisation|colonial empires]] and remaining military dictatorships in [[Portuguese transition to democracy|Portugal]], [[Greek junta|Greece]] and [[Spanish transition to democracy|Spain]] were dismantled. After Soviet leader [[Mikhail Gorbachev]] refused to intervene militarily against [[1989 Polish parliamentary election|democratic reforms in Poland]] and [[Sinatra Doctrine|elsewhere]], the [[Revolutions of 1989]] led to all European communist states [[transition economy|transitioning]] to [[Market economy|market economies]] and then the [[Dissolution of the Soviet Union|Soviet Union itself dissolving]]. The [[European Union]] ("EU") was established in 1993. In the 21st century, most of the former communist states [[Expansion of the European Union|joined the EU]] and Europe faced the [[Euro area crisis]] (2009–2018) and the [[2022 Russian invasion of Ukraine|Russian invasion of Ukraine]] (2022–).

