{{TopicTOC-Biology}}

In [[biology]], '''homeostasis''' (in [[British English]] also '''homoeostasis''') is the process by which living [[organism]]s maintain relatively stable internal physical and chemical conditions despite changes in their internal or external environment.<ref>{{cite book |title=Anatomy and physiology |last=Betts |first=J. Gordon |last2=DeSaix |first2=Peter |last3=Johnson |first3=Eddie |last4=Johnson |first4=Jody E. |last5=Korol |first5=Oksana |last6=Kruse |first6=Dean H. |last7=Poe |first7=Brandon |isbn=978-1-947172-04-3 |location=Houston, Texas |publisher=OpenStax |page=9 |oclc=1001472383}}</ref> [[Body temperature]], [[blood sugar level|blood glucose]], [[blood pressure]], [[fluid balance]], levels of [[oxygen]] and [[carbon dioxide]] in the blood, [[pH]], and the concentrations of ions such as [[sodium]], [[potassium]], and [[calcium]] are all homeostatically regulated variables. These variables are generally maintained within physiological ranges rather than at absolutely constant values; [[Setpoint (control system)|set points]] for some regulated variables may also change, as occurs with circadian variation in body temperature or during [[fever]].

Homeostatic regulation commonly operates through [[negative feedback]]. Sensors detect changes in a regulated variable, control mechanisms coordinate an appropriate response, and [[Effector cell|effectors]] act to counter the deviation.<ref name=Marieb/> Regulation can involve the [[nervous system]], [[endocrine system]], and organs: the [[kidney]]s, [[lung]]s, [[liver]], and [[pancreas]], as well as cellular mechanisms such as the [[regulation of gene expression]]. [[Predictive homeostasis]] consists of anticipatory responses to expected changes, but these require feedback mechanisms to correct deviations. Together, homeostatic processes maintain conditions in which [[metabolism]] and other physiological functions can occur.

The concept of regulation of the internal environment was developed in the 19th century by the French physiologist [[Claude Bernard]], and the term ''homeostasis'' was coined by the American physiologist [[Walter Bradford Cannon]] in 1926. Failure of homeostatic mechanisms can contribute to disease; examples include [[diabetes mellitus type 1|type 1 diabetes]], disorders of calcium regulation, and disturbances of water balance. The effectiveness of homeostatic control can also decline with aging, and compensatory mechanisms may temporarily mask chronic disease.

Although primarily a biological concept, homeostasis has also been applied to the [[biosphere]] and, by analogy, in a range of other fields.

